1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,055
Filling a weekly pill organizer is easy to get wrong.

2
00:00:03,055 --> 00:00:06,686
Pillbox Guide shows which compartment to use, right on the
box.

3
00:00:06,686 --> 00:00:13,884
This video is a slideshow of screenshots from the Lens Studio
preview, because my laptop could not record live.

4
00:00:13,884 --> 00:00:18,297
First, the grid is placed over the organizer, which here is a
stand-in box.

5
00:00:18,297 --> 00:00:23,475
The user pinches and drags the grid into place, stretches it
to match, and presses Done.

6
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:26,829
In my tests the hand input is simulated by a test script.

7
00:00:26,829 --> 00:00:31,159
How well the grid stays aligned on real glasses is unproven.

8
00:00:31,159 --> 00:00:36,003
A schedule card comes first, with a reminder to check the list
with your pharmacist.

9
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:37,790
Then each medicine gets a step.

10
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:43,326
Every compartment where it goes lights up with a one, and the
bright one with the arrow is next.

11
00:00:43,326 --> 00:00:44,536
The card always says:

12
00:00:44,536 --> 00:00:47,470
Example schedule, not medical advice.

13
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:53,188
Pinching Next, or anywhere, ticks the compartment green with a
check and moves the arrow on.

14
00:00:53,188 --> 00:00:54,493
The card keeps count.

15
00:00:54,493 --> 00:00:56,536
Undo steps back one.

16
00:00:56,536 --> 00:01:03,362
After the last compartment of a medicine, the card says to put
the box away, then moves on to the next medicine.

17
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:12,349
At the end, every compartment shows how many tablets it should
hold, for one last check, and the card says the organizer is
ready, with the pharmacist reminder.

18
00:01:12,349 --> 00:01:14,372
The Lens never gives medical advice.

19
00:01:14,372 --> 00:01:17,362
It only shows the schedule you give it.

20
00:01:17,362 --> 00:01:22,652
Built with Claude Code and CLAD, and checked with six LEAF
tests in the Lens Studio preview.

21
00:01:22,652 --> 00:01:26,672
This was only tried in the preview, not on real glasses.

